think Crux pick: the subscript-digits corpus landed last lap (nib 7cd38076ad, rota item 5). Its exact companion is the SUPERSCRIPT digits -- the twin from the other side of the line. High coherence with the just-finished work, small and tractable, genuinely Lindy (an open font read for years).
think The distinguishing crux over subscript: superscript codepoints are famously NON-contiguous -- one/two/three sit in the Latin-1 Supplement (U+00B9,U+00B2,U+00B3), zero and four-through-nine in the superscripts block (U+2070,U+2074..U+2079). So the corpus carries an explicit ascending table (greek's pattern) rather than base-derivation (subscript's pattern), and the digit order is scrambled against codepoint order (opens on the digit two). Proved both on metal.
think Lap index math: geometric read rota item 27, then boxarc=1, math=2, currency=3, greek=4, subscript=5 -- so this lap reads item 6, foundations/20260814-071700_hardware-and-right-to-repair.md. Read it: Season A stands proven in software and waits on honest metal; this corpus is more open font content proven before any board.
obs Mirrored the subscript twin exactly: same digit shapes moved from the bottom five rows to the top five, the blank-rows check flipped from top-two to bottom-two. Reused greek's explicit codepoint-table shape for the non-contiguous run. Ten digits, ink 7..13 of 35, eight>one holds, all raised, table non-contiguous and scrambled.
obs GREEN on first metal run, no red. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII (numeric codepoint literals -- the ascii-first font-table exception). README lagged math/currency/greek/subscript; stayed bounded (ratchet not sweep) and added one coherent subscript+superscript twin bullet rather than backfilling all four.
